### Endocrine System - Summary

**Thyroid:** Nodular hyperplasia (multinodular goiter) shows multiple unencapsulated nodules with variable-sized follicles and no nuclear atypia. Follicular adenoma is a solitary encapsulated nodule without capsular or vascular invasion. Papillary carcinoma is diagnosed by nuclear features (Orphan Annie eyes, nuclear grooves, pseudoinclusions, psammoma bodies). Follicular carcinoma requires capsular or vascular invasion for diagnosis. Anaplastic carcinoma shows extreme pleomorphism and aggressive behavior. Medullary carcinoma (C-cell origin) features amyloid stroma and salt-and-pepper chromatin. Hashimoto thyroiditis shows dense lymphoid infiltration with Hurthle cell metaplasia. Graves disease features diffuse hyperplasia with tall columnar cells and scalloped colloid.

**Parathyroid:** Adenoma is a well-circumscribed lesion with chief cell proliferation and compressed surrounding parathyroid tissue.

**Pituitary:** Pituitary adenoma (PitNET) shows monotonous cell population with disrupted reticulin network. ACTH-producing tumors may show Crooke hyaline change in adjacent normal pituitary.

**Adrenal:** Adrenocortical carcinoma shows high mitotic activity, atypical mitoses, and capsular/vascular invasion. Pheochromocytoma features Zellballen pattern with S-100 positive sustentacular cells. Neuroblastoma presents as a small round blue cell tumor with Homer-Wright pseudorosettes.

**Endocrine Pancreas:** Insulinoma is the most common pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor, well-circumscribed with salt-and-pepper chromatin. In diabetes mellitus, Type 1 shows insulitis (lymphocytic infiltration) while Type 2 shows islet amyloid deposition.
